# 94_tenant_vs_tenant — TWO PORTED CHARACTERS IN ONE MATCH (14z-95).
#
# WHY IT EXISTS. CLAUDE.md §4 mandates "vs each of the 18 (both sides)" for a
# ported character, and until now `tests/replays/` contained NO pairing of two
# ported characters at all — the gap GitHub #99 walked through. The arcade
# marathon cannot cover it: it is a single-credit soak that plays ONE
# character, and (measured 14z-95) it reaches only two ladder rungs.
#
# The replay is character-AGNOSTIC on purpose. It carries no tenant-specific
# motion, so ONE script serves all six orderings and the consumer chooses the
# pairing with the standard forced-pick pokes ($FF8782 / $FF8B82). Adding a
# tenant means adding a row to the gate's list, not authoring a replay.
#
# WHAT IT DOES: both sides walk in, trade normals of three strengths, throw a
# quarter-circle each way, jump, and walk back out. Deliberately ordinary —
# this rig exists to prove a tenant-vs-tenant match FORMS and SURVIVES, not to
# characterise any move. Exotic inputs belong in the per-tenant rigs, which
# already exist; what did not exist was the pairing itself.
#
# BOTH ORDERINGS ARE RUN BY THE CONSUMER, not just both characters. P1 and P2
# are different structs reached by different code paths — the 14z-69 note
# "poke BOTH sides, the cursor lands on different characters on the two
# wheels" is the same lesson — so D-vs-H and H-vs-D are two tests, not one.
